# metaparser-js

RE:INSTEAD (METAPARSER 3) in a browser: WebAssembly plus a small JS glue.

## Build

Only Emscripten is required. Lua comes from `../../src/lua`, zlib is built by
Emscripten itself (the `USE_ZLIB` port), so nothing has to be downloaded.

    EMSDK=/path/to/emsdk ./build.sh

If `emcc` is already in `PATH`, use make directly:

    make            # wasm + js fallback + build/dist
    make wasm       # WebAssembly only
    make js         # JS fallback only (WASM=0, deprecated but working)
    make smoke      # check under node, no browser
    make serve      # serve build/dist locally
    make clean

The result is in `build/dist`:

    index.html                 loader page
    lib/*                      jquery slim, mp.js, mp.css, favicon
    metaparser-wasm.{js,wasm,data}
    metaparser-js.{js,data}    fallback without WebAssembly

`build.sh smoke` additionally builds a modular node version and runs the
demo game (`data/games/trial`) to make sure the parser answers. It needs no
browser, so it is handy for CI.

## Running

Files must be served over HTTP: a `file://` page is not allowed to load the
game.

    cd build/dist && python3 -m http.server 8000

The game is taken from `data.zip` next to `index.html`, or from a link such
as `https://…/index.html?mygame.zip`, or from the meta tag in `index.html`:

    <meta name="gamefile" content="yourgame.zip">

If you want the WASM build only, drop `metaparser-js.*` and replace the WASM
detector in `index.html` with `<script async src="metaparser-wasm.js"></script>`.

## Build notes

* `compat/` holds small shim headers (`externals.h`, `utils.h`, `ints.h`)
  that the minizip part is missing: `unpack.c`, `ioapi.h` and `unzip.c` came
  from a project with a different directory layout.
* minizip is built with `-DNOUNCRYPT`: game archives are not password
  protected, and `crypt.c` is not part of this tree.
* `-DGAMES_PATH="/games"` is where an uploaded zip is unpacked.
* `morph/morphs.mrd` (the full 8 MB dictionary) is left out of the image:
  games ship their own `dict.mrd`.
